SUE Frequently Asked Questions:

Q: Are my SUE files corrupted if my machine crashes.

A: When SUE netlists to either spice or verilog, it creates cache
   files so that subsequent netlisting is faster.  These files can
   get corrupted so it is a good idea to do a:

	rm *.cache tclIndex

   Note that is is also a good idea to delete these cache files
   when upgrading to a new version of SUE or if something weird
   happens when running SUE.


Q: What do I do if I want to netlist a schematic in a directory that I
   don't have write permission into?

A: Netlisting information goes into the same directory as where the 
   schematic came from.  Do a "change path of cell" in the "File" menu
   to change the schematic's path to a place where you do have write
   permission and netlist again.


Q: Every time I bring up a certain schematic, it gives me an older 
   version of it even though I keep fixing it and saving it out.
   What gives?

A: You probably did a unix copy of the schematic to give it another
   name.  For example you copied foo.sue to be foo_old.sue.  Because
   both of these files have the .sue suffix, SUE looks into both of 
   them and sees the schematic called foo and gets confused.  
   Solution: don't use unix cp/mv to change the name of a cell (changing
   its directory is ok).  To see what potential problems you have
   do a "check file system" command from the "File" menu.


Q: When I netlist, I get the wrong syntax for one of my I/O's.  What's
   wrong?

A: You probably named one of your I/O's to be the same name as one
   of the "user" properties.  These are the names of the headings in 
   the "edit properties" menu that you get when you double-click on
   an icon and they are defined in the icon view of that schematic.
   For example, you might have called the port "M" but you have a
   user property of "M" for the spice multiplier.  Solution:  either
   rename the I/O or remove/rename the user property.


Q: The Pearl static timing analyser, when running SUE DPC, gives the
   error:

	Error: possibility number must be 1:2

   or similar.  What does this mean?

A: Pearl has been instructed to report the top "n" (default is in
   DPC_TIMING(paths)) critical paths.  If there are fewer than "n"
   paths in the circuit, Pearl will give this error message.  In a
   purely combinational circuits, Pearl thinks there are only as
   many paths as outputs (don't ask me why).

   Note: you can safely ignore this error.


Q: The wires don't line up exactly with the icon ports in the schematic.
   What happened and how do I fix this?

A: Sometimes (usually due to a bug in SUE) the icons get "off grid".  To
   fix this, go to the schematic with the off grid problem and type:

	fix_off_grid

   in the SUE command window.  This should fix the off grid icons
   (remember to save the fixes).  Note that this fix will sometimes 
   disconnect some wires that you need to then manually fix.


Q: Can I run SUE in the background?

A: No, SUE must run in the foreground.  Note that if you pause SUE
   with Ctrl-Z, placing it in the background, the window won't even
   repaint until you place it back into the foreground.  Furthermore,
   the Ctrl-C and Ctrl-D functions in the SUE command window (now a
   unix window) have been disabled and won't be re-enabled until you
   exit normally from SUE.


Q: Why do I sometimes get a SUE ERROR when netlisting or other operation?

A: If you hit the hotkey for an operation twice in a row, you will get
   an error.  Be careful not to repeat hotkeys or hold them down which
   causes auto repeat.


Q: SUE is producing strange, inconsistent behavior.  Sometimes libraries
   don't get autoloaded.  What gives?

A: If any of the disks that SUE accesses through any libraries are full,
   SUE will choke since it needs to write temporary files (in particular
   the tclIndex file).  Check if your disks are full, and if so, make some
   room on them.


Q: SUE seems to put out verilog syntax that my tool can't read.  What 
   syntax is bad and why?

A: The full verilog definition is incredibly complicated (some would
   say "rich"), especially dealing with behavioral/RTL code.  Verilog
   is often used as a structural netlist format for tools like
   place & route and static timing analysis.  Hence any behavioral
   code is off limits in these applications.  The question of what
   syntax exactly is ok for these tools is, unfortunately, not
   consistent.  The problems usually come down to two problems:

	A. Use of the "assign" statement
	B. Legal/Illegal characters in names and nets

   The "assign" statement is particularly problematic.  We have
   seen tools with some or all of the following restrictions:

	1. Doesn't allow use of the assign statement at all
	2. Doesn't allow outputs to be assigned to internal nets
	3. Doesn't allow outputs to be assigned to inputs
	4. Doesn't recognize the multiplier syntax, e.g.: 4{GND}
	5. Doesn't allow left hand concatenation, e.g.:
		assign {a,b} = c;

   Most tools seem to allow 1 and 2.  Many tools break on
   3, 4, and/or 5.  The syntax of 5 is created by the bus_split 
   icon (might be removed from your library).  The bus_combine 
   icon creates the most tolerant form of assign.	

   Bottom line: don't use bus_split.


Q: When trying to save a file, SUE aborts, giving me an error that says
   there is a newer sccs version than the one I have.  How can
   I save this file?

A: SUE is trying to tell you that someone else (or possibly you in
   a concurrent version of SUE) has modified the cell you are in and
   saved it back.  Therefore, your version is out of date and saving
   it will wipe out someone else's edits.

   What to do?  If you are sure that you want to save this cell as is,
   just change the version number in the sccs_title_bar (you might
   have to add one first) to the most recent version and save again.

   Otherwise, SUE copy the cell to another name.  In a unix window,
   check out the most recent version of the cell.  In SUE, revert
   the cell and add your changes into it, and then save.


Q: When I try to run SUE, I get a "word too long" error and SUE doesn't come
   up.  What went wrong?

A: Your font path is too long.  To fix, type:

	xset fp default


Q: SUE's netlister isn't correctly generating buses when I use name nets.
   What am I doing wrong?

A: Don't put name nets directly on ports of instances, I/O's,
   bus_combines, etc.  Move them off at least one grid.  This should
   fix the problem.


Q: What's the difference between name_net, name_net_s, and name_net_sw?

A: There is no functional difference.  They just provide a different look
   to the schematics.


Q: SUE won't run external programs like verilog or chmod.  SUE says
   that they aren't in my unix path, but when I check my path, they
   are there.  Why can't SUE find them?

A: Your path probably includes some non-directories in it.  There is
   a TCL bug that if an element in the path is not a directory, it
   barfs.  Check your path and make sure that each entry is a directory.

   Another possibility is that your path is too long and that when
   you start up SUE, you get the message:

		ridiculously long path, truncating

   In this case you need to shorten your path.


Q: Is it possible to cut text out of another unix window and
   paste it into a SUE schematic?

A: You can paste into popup windows in SUE.  For example, if you 
   double click on an icon, SUE will popup a window to edit its 
   properties.  You can cut and paste into this window.  

   You can't paste text directly into a text item in SUE, i.e. one
   that you get to from the "add text" menu command in the "Edit" menu.
   However, you can paste into the name of an icon, for example an
   input icon, and then use the "duplicate text" menu command in the
   "Edit" menu to turn it into a text item.


Q: In SUE DPC, How do I specify to Pathmill that a node is an input
   but that no critical paths should result from it, e.g. like the
   scan enable pin?

A: You need to add a line into the .constraint file of the form:

	no_path_node scan_enable

   You can't use the line:

	input_node scan_enable

   Because DPC has already defined a source node for scan_enable:

	source_node scan_enable slope=0.2 delay=0.5 clock=a r clk

   since it is an input and this overrides the input_node statment
   in Pathmill.  


Q: How can I get SUE to stop giving me warnings during netlisting
   on unconnected ports that I really want to be unconnected?

A: Put a name_net on the port.  If you don't put a name on the
   name_net, SUE will make one up (like net_2).  If you do put a name
   on it like "unconnected", remember that the port will get connected to 
   any other ports named "unconnected" -- which is probably not
   what you want.

